Below is an example of how you might structure data based on a generic JSON schema that could be standardized across various applications or systems. This example includes a generic dataset with a few fields (`id`, `name`, `description`, `categories`) and a more complex example with nested objects (`user`, `address`, `orders`). The key part here is defining a schema that can be standardized and then customizing data output based on that schema.

### JSON Schema Definition

Let's define a basic schema that can be used to structure various types of data. This schema includes a flexible way to describe data with fields and nested objects.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
  "title": "Extended JSON Schema",
  "description": "A basic structure for data with fields and nested objects.",
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "id": {"type": "integer"},
    "name": {"type": "string"},
    "description": {"type": "string"},
    "created_at": {"type": "string", "format": "date"},
    "modified_at": {"type": "string", "format": "date"},
    "fields": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "field_name": {"type": "string"},
          "field_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["text", "number", "boolean"]}
        },
        "required": ["field_name", "field_type"]
      }
    },
    "nested": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "oneOf": [
          {"$ref": "#/definitions/nestedObject"},
          {"$ref": "#/definitions/nestedArray"}
        ]
      }
    }
  },
  "definitions": {
    "nestedObject": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "id": {"type": "integer"},
        "name": {"type": "string"}
      }
    },
    "nestedArray": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {"type": "integer"}
    }
  }
}
```

### Example Output

Now, let's populate this schema with data. Below, you'll see how to structure the output based on the schema.

```json
{
  "id": 1,
  "name": "Example Data",
  "description": "Data example",
  "created_at": "2022-01-01",
  "modified_at": "2022-01-01",
  "fields": {
    "address": {"field_name": "Address", "field_type": "text"},
    "phone": {"field_name": "Phone", "field_type": "number"}
  },
  "nested": [
    {"id": 1, "name": "Nested Object 1"},
    [1, 2, 3]  // A nested array
  ]
}
```

### Customizing Data Output

This approach allows for flexibility in data output. For example, to output data as JSON-LD (Linked Data), you'd start with the schema and then customize how the properties are represented as JSON-LD vocabularies or as simple JSON properties with no additional structure.

This method not only helps standardize data formats across platforms but also facilitates data transformation and integration, especially when working with datasets that need to conform to specific schema requirements.
